COURSE_DATA("dp",{"key":"dp","module":"DP","chapters":[{"id":"dp-1","title":"Chapter 9 (1) — Data Protection: Policies, Organization and Processing Activities","title_en":"Data Protection (1): Policies, Organization and Processing Activities","intro":["Data Protection is Chapter 9 of the ISA. It does not replace the information security controls; it **supplements** them. The original text says this right at the start: the Data Protection tab supplements the Information Security tab, and to obtain an effective TISAX assessment of data protection, the information security questionnaire must also be assessed. Therefore, **an isolated assessment of the data protection module cannot lead to a TISAX label**. The Definitions tab also explains that this tab only has to be completed when you carry out “processing within the meaning of Art. 28 GDPR” (that is, processing personal data on behalf of a customer), and that the controls are answered only with yes or no.","First, get the two roles clear. Under Art. 4(7) GDPR, the **controller** is the party that, alone or jointly with others, determines the “purposes and means” of processing personal data. Under Art. 4(8), the **processor** is the party that processes personal data on behalf of the controller. Art. 28 says the controller may only use processors that provide sufficient guarantees, the two parties must sign a contract with the content required by law (usually called a DPA, Data Processing Agreement), and the processor may only process personal data on the controller's “documented instructions”. ISA Chapter 9 asks its questions from the **processor's** point of view: as a service provider, are you able to take proper care of the personal data your customer entrusts to you?","Why are suppliers outside the EU also asked these questions? Because many suppliers, wherever they are located, process EU personal data for German and other European OEMs and Tier-1s. Examples: names and driving-behavior records of test drivers in road-test vehicles; names, e-mail addresses and accounts of the customer's engineers (PLM, collaboration platforms, ticket systems); connected-vehicle data (VIN, GPS tracks, in-cabin camera images); and vehicle-owner data in after-sales or customer-service systems. As long as you process this data on behalf of the OEM, you are a processor under the GDPR, and the OEM will ask you to pass the TISAX data protection objectives.","In practice, among the ENX TISAX assessment objectives, the data protection labels people hear about most are **Data** (processing of ordinary personal data) and **Special data** (processing of special categories of personal data under Art. 9 GDPR, such as health data, biometric data, religious beliefs, trade-union membership, racial or ethnic origin, genetic data, sex life or sexual orientation). These two labels are usually assessed at a higher assessment level and are always assessed together with the information security controls. Which objective and assessment level you actually need depends on your OEM's requirements and the latest ENX Participant Handbook.","Also note that the controls in this chapter **only have Must requirements. There are no Should requirements, no additional requirements for high or very high protection needs, and no maturity levels**. The result is simply “fulfilled” or “not fulfilled”. So you prepare differently than for the information security controls: you do not have to prove that you reach Level 3, but for every Must you need to show concrete documents and records."],"blocks":[{"type":"h3","text":"Controller and processor: first find out who you are"},{"type":"table","head":["Item","Controller","Processor"],"rows":[["GDPR definition","Art. 4(7): determines the purposes and means of processing","Art. 4(8): processes personal data on behalf of the controller"],["Typical role in the automotive industry","OEM or Tier-1 (the customer that places the order)","Suppliers providing testing, software development, data analysis, cloud operations or customer service"],["Main obligations","Legal basis, information to data subjects, responding to data subjects, notifying the supervisory authority within 72 hours, DPIA","Processing only on instructions, confidentiality, security measures, assisting the controller, notifying the controller of incidents, managing sub-processors"],["Position in ISA Chapter 9","The party that sets requirements and audits","The party that is assessed and answers yes or no"]]},{"type":"callout","style":"info","title":"Common TISAX data protection labels","text":"**Data**: processing of ordinary personal data within the meaning of Art. 28 GDPR. **Special data**: processing of special categories of personal data under Art. 9 GDPR. Both require the information security controls to be assessed at the same time; an isolated data protection assessment cannot lead to a TISAX label. For the assessment objective and level, follow your OEM's requirements and the latest ENX documents."},{"type":"h3","text":"Supplement: your local data-protection law vs. GDPR"},{"type":"table","head":["Concept","GDPR","Your local data-protection law (example: Taiwan PDPA)"],"rows":[["Party that places the order","Controller","Find the term your law uses for the party that commissions processing (Taiwan PDPA: “commissioning agency”)"],["Party that carries out the order","Processor","Check whether your law treats the service provider as a separate role or as the controller (Taiwan PDPA Art. 4: within the scope of the law, the commissioned party is deemed to be the commissioning agency)"],["Supervision of the service provider","Art. 28 contract and audit rights","Check whether your law requires the customer to supervise the provider (Taiwan PDPA Enforcement Rules Art. 8: appropriate supervision, with clearly agreed supervision items)"],["Processing only on instructions","Art. 28(3)(a) and Art. 29","Check whether your law limits the provider to the customer's instructions (Taiwan PDPA Enforcement Rules Art. 7: collection, processing and use only within the scope of the instructions)"],["Incident notification","Controller notifies the supervisory authority within 72 hours; the processor notifies the controller without undue delay","Check who must be notified and by when (Taiwan PDPA Art. 12: notify the data subjects in an appropriate way after the facts are clarified)"]]},{"type":"callout","style":"warn","title":"Whose duty is the 72-hour deadline?","text":"The 72-hour notification to the supervisory authority in Art. 33(1) GDPR is the duty of the **controller**. As a processor, under Art. 33(2) you must notify the controller **without undue delay** after becoming aware of a personal data breach. In practice, OEM DPAs often agree on a shorter deadline, for example 24 or 48 hours. Always follow your contract."},{"type":"tip","who":"allan","text":"Many suppliers I have coached start with the same sentence: “We don't have any personal data from European customers.” Then we check, and we find PLM accounts, test-vehicle driving logs and the customer engineers' e-mail addresses. Take an inventory first, then decide whether you really have none."}],"controls":[{"id":"9.1.1","title":"Data protection policies","q_en":"To what extent do data protection policies exist?","q_zh":"To what extent do data protection policies exist?","objective":"The organization needs at least one policy on privacy. This reflects the importance and significance of data protection and is adapted to the organization. Other policies may be appropriate depending on the size and structure of your organization.","req":{"must":["A policy is created, regularly updated, and approved by the organization's management."],"should":[],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"No reference standards listed in the original | Supplementary mapping: GDPR Art. 5(2) accountability, Art. 24(2), Art. 32 | ISO/IEC 27701 6.2, ISO 27001:2022 A.5.1, A.5.34","change2027":"","explain":["The Must in 9.1.1 is only one sentence, but it contains three elements: **created**, **regularly updated** and **approved by management**. The auditor does not only check whether a document exists. They check whether it has an approval record (signature, meeting minutes or an electronic approval workflow), whether the version history shows a recent review, and whether the content really fits the organization. Many companies simply download a template from the internet that still contains another company's name or departments that do not exist in their organization. This is a typical case of a policy that was “not adapted to the organization”.","The objective mentions “at least one policy on privacy” and adds that “other policies may be appropriate depending on size and structure”. For small and medium-sized suppliers, a common approach is to add a “Personal Data Protection Policy” to the ISMS document system, supported by a few procedures, for example a “Personal Data Inventory and Record of Processing Activities Procedure”, a “Data Subject Request Handling Procedure”, a “Personal Data Breach Notification Procedure” and an “Outsourcing and Sub-processor Management Procedure”. The policy sets the direction and commitment; the procedures describe how to do it.","Note that the policy must cover the scenario of “processing customer personal data on the customer's behalf”, not only the privacy notice for website visitors. The website privacy notice is a document in which you, as controller, inform the public. It is different from the “internal data protection policy” that TISAX looks at. Both can exist side by side, but you cannot use the website notice as a substitute.","The policy should also be consistent with the information security policy (ISA 1.1.1). For example, it is best to use the same document management approach for review frequency, approval level and publication. This makes it easier to explain everything in one go during the assessment."],"practice":["Create a “Personal Data Protection Policy” that covers at least: scope (including processing of customer personal data on the customer's behalf), applicable laws (GDPR, your national data-protection law and customer contracts), data protection principles (lawfulness, purpose limitation, data minimization, accuracy, storage limitation, integrity and confidentiality, accountability), roles and responsibilities, and consequences of violations.","Have the policy approved by the managing director or the chairman, keep evidence of the approval through an electronic approval system or management review minutes, and show the version, approval date and next review date on the first page.","Review the policy at least once a year, and also carry out an ad-hoc review when laws change, customer DPA requirements change or a major personal data breach occurs.","Publish the policy on the intranet (e.g. SharePoint, Google Sites) and include it in onboarding training. If needed, provide an English version for OEM auditors or customers.","Add lower-level procedures depending on the size of the organization, and include a document structure diagram in the policy so that the auditor can see at a glance how the policy and the procedures relate.","Set up a document review log with the columns: document name, version, review date, reviewer, summary of changes, approver."],"audit":{"questions":["Does your company have a data protection or personal data protection policy? Please provide the latest version.","Who approved this policy? Where is the approval record?","How often is it updated? What changed in the most recent revision?","Does the policy cover personal data processed on behalf of customers, such as OEM test data or engineer accounts?","How do employees learn about this policy? Are there announcement or training records?","How does this policy relate to the information security policy?"],"evidence":["Latest version of the Personal Data Protection Policy, including version history","Management approval record (approval form, screenshot of electronic approval, management review minutes)","Document review log","Screenshot of the internal announcement or employee read-and-acknowledge records","List of lower-level procedures"],"findings":["The policy is an internet template used as is; it contains another company's name or departments that do not exist, and it was not adapted to the organization.","There is no evidence of management approval, or the approver is the IT manager instead of management.","The policy was last revised many years ago, there are no review records, and regular updates cannot be shown.","The website privacy notice is used instead of an internal policy and does not cover the processing of customer personal data on the customer's behalf."]},"tip":"The policy does not need to read like a legal thesis, but the auditor must see three things: it is written about your company, the boss has signed it, and someone has reviewed it recently."},{"id":"9.2.1","title":"Data protection organization and responsibilities","q_en":"To what extent are the responsibilities for data protection organized?","q_zh":"To what extent are the responsibilities for data protection organized?","objective":"Successful data protection requires clear responsibilities in the organization.","req":{"must":["A data protection officer is appointed, if required by Art. 37 GDPR – Determination of whether the appointment of a data protection officer is voluntary or mandatory – Otherwise, determination of a data protection function or comparable","Publication of contact details (e.g. on the Internet)","Integration into the organization's structure","Exercise of the control obligations as defined in Art. 39 (1) (b) GDPR and corresponding documentation","Documentation of the data protection status and report to the organization's top management","Equipped with sufficient capacities and resources – Determination of whether the data protection function is full-time or part-time – Adequate professional qualification – Regular professional training – Access to specialist literature – Support of the data protection officer by data protection coordinators in the company's organizational units, depending on the company size (e.g. marketing, sales, personnel, logistics, development, etc.)"],"should":[],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"No reference standards listed in the original | Supplementary mapping: GDPR Art. 37, 38, 39 | ISO/IEC 27701 6.3.1, ISO 27001:2022 A.5.2, A.5.34","change2027":"","explain":["The key word in the first Must is “determination”. Art. 37 GDPR requires a DPO to be appointed in certain cases, for example when the core activities require regular and systematic monitoring of data subjects on a large scale, or when the core activities consist of large-scale processing of special categories of personal data. Companies in the EU must also check national laws; for example, the German BDSG has its own thresholds. The ISA does not require you to have a DPO in every case. It requires that you **have made a determination and that the determination is recorded**. If the appointment is not mandatory, you must still assign a data protection function or a comparable role.","For suppliers outside the EU, whether Art. 37 GDPR applies must be decided case by case. We recommend asking your legal department or an external advisor for a short written determination that describes your processing activities, their scale, whether special categories of personal data are involved, and the conclusion. Even when the appointment is not mandatory, most companies still assign a “data protection lead” or set up a data protection team. This is what the requirement calls a “data protection function or comparable”.","The other Musts make this role “come alive”: the contact details must be published (for example on the website or in the DPA annex for customers); the role must be part of the organization chart, not just a name on paper; it must perform the monitoring described in Art. 39(1)(b), that is, monitoring compliance with the law and internal policies, including the assignment of responsibilities, awareness-raising and training of staff, and related audits, with records kept; and it must regularly report the data protection status to top management.","Finally, resources: state clearly whether the role is full-time or part-time; the person must have professional qualifications and receive regular training; they must have access to specialist literature (for example the GDPR text, EDPB guidelines and legal databases); and in larger companies, each department should have a data protection coordinator to support the DPO. Auditors often ask: how much time does this person spend on data protection per year? When was their last training?"],"practice":["Prepare a “DPO Appointment Determination Record” that lists the basis (each case in Art. 37 GDPR, processing scale, whether special categories of data are involved), the conclusion, who made the determination and when. Review it every year or when the business changes.","Formally appoint the DPO or data protection lead with a letter of appointment signed by the managing director, stating the responsibilities, authority, full-time or part-time status, and the right to report directly to top management.","Publish the data protection contact on the privacy page of your website or in the DPA annex for customers. Use a role-based mailbox such as privacy@yourdomain so that the contact is not lost when people change jobs.","Show the data protection function in the organization chart and the ISMS responsibility matrix (RACI), and appoint data protection coordinators in HR, R&D, sales, IT and other units.","Set up an annual monitoring plan: internal data protection audits, spot checks of the record of processing activities, and checks of training completion rates. Keep the audit reports and follow-up records.","Every quarter or every six months, submit a “Data Protection Status Report” to management covering incidents, data subject requests, customer audit results, training completion rates and open improvement items. This can be part of the ISMS management review.","Plan a budget for training and resources for the data protection lead, for example GDPR or national data-protection law courses, relevant certifications and a subscription to a legal database."],"audit":{"questions":["Does your company have to appoint a DPO under Art. 37 GDPR? What is the basis for your decision?","Who is responsible for data protection? Is it a full-time or part-time role? Please provide the appointment document.","How can external parties or customers contact the data protection function?","How does the responsible person carry out monitoring? Please provide the most recent monitoring or audit record.","How often is the data protection status reported to top management? Please provide the report.","When did the responsible person last receive professional training?"],"evidence":["DPO appointment determination record","Letter of appointment or job description","Screenshot of the contact details published on the website or in the DPA","Organization chart and responsibility matrix","Monitoring plan and internal audit reports","Data protection status report to management or management review minutes","Training records or certificates of the responsible person"],"findings":["No one has ever determined whether a DPO is required, and no data protection function has been assigned.","The responsible person exists in name only: there are no monitoring records and no reports to management.","The published contact is the personal mailbox of an employee who has left the company.","The role is held by an IT staff member who has had no professional data protection training and cannot describe their own responsibilities."]},"tip":"The answer I fear most is: “Data protection lead? Probably Legal.” Then Legal says it is IT, and IT says it is HR. The appointment must be in black and white, and reports must be delivered regularly. Otherwise, the role does not exist."},{"id":"9.3.1","title":"Record of processing activities","q_en":"To what extent are processing activities identified and recorded?","q_zh":"To what extent are processing activities identified and recorded?","objective":"The company fulfils its duty of accountability and transparency and thus creates an overview of the respective data processing.","req":{"must":["If required by law, a register of processing activities as defined in Article 30 (1) and/or (2) GDPR (in the latter case only information relating to the order, expressly not other information or details on internal processing) exists and is up to date. – Technical and organizational measures required for processing as required by the information security questionnaire are adequately implemented for the processing activities – There is a process description or sequence description with defined responsibilities."],"should":[],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"No reference standards listed in the original | Supplementary mapping: GDPR Art. 30, Art. 32 | ISO/IEC 27701 7.2.8, 8.2.6, ISO 27001:2022 A.5.9, A.5.34","change2027":"","explain":["Art. 30(1) GDPR covers the controller's record of processing activities, and Art. 30(2) covers the processor's record. ISA 9.3.1 states explicitly that for Art. 30(2) (processor), the record only needs to contain information relating to the order and **expressly not** other details of the company's internal processing. In other words, the data you process for the OEM must be listed item by item, while your own HR payroll processing belongs to your Art. 30(1) record as controller. Keep the two separate.","Under Art. 30(2), the processor's record usually contains: the name and contact details of the processor and of each controller (and their DPOs), the categories of processing carried out on behalf of each controller, transfers to third countries and documentation of suitable safeguards, and a general description of the technical and organizational security measures. The requirement starts with “if required by law”: Art. 30(5) GDPR provides an exemption for organizations with fewer than 250 employees, but the exemption does not apply if the processing is not occasional, is likely to result in a risk to data subjects, or includes special categories of data. Processing customer data on the customer's behalf is usually ongoing, so in practice you almost always need a record.","The two sub-points are also important. First, the technical and organizational measures (TOMs) required for each processing activity must be properly implemented as required by the information security questionnaire. This means the record of processing activities must link to the controls in ISA Chapters 1 to 7. Second, there must be a process or sequence description with defined responsibilities. The record is not just an Excel sheet: you must be able to explain where the data comes from, which systems it passes through, who is responsible and when it is deleted.","The example in the original text also reminds you that you do not have to list every information asset individually; you can form categories, such as “employee master data – responsible office: Human Resources Department”. For processing on behalf of customers, you can use “test-driver data for project X of OEM Y” as one processing category, without going down to every single file."],"practice":["Set up two Records of Processing Activities (RoPA): one as controller (Art. 30(1)) and one as processor (Art. 30(2)). The processor record is organized by customer and order.","Suggested columns for the processor record: customer name and contact person, customer DPO, related contract or DPA number, category and purpose of processing, categories of personal data and data subjects, whether special categories of data are included, systems used and storage location, sub-processors, third-country transfers and safeguards, retention and deletion periods, ISA controls that implement the TOMs, and business owner.","Attach a data flow diagram to each order, showing where data enters, is processed, is output and is deleted, together with the responsible persons.","Link the RoPA to the information asset inventory (ISA 1.3.1) and the protection needs classification, so that personal data with high protection needs has matching encryption, access control and logging.","Define update triggers: new project start, contract change, new sub-processor or system, project closure. Review the whole RoPA at least every six months.","Manage the RoPA in Excel on an access-controlled SharePoint site, or in a GRC tool such as OneTrust, and keep the version history."],"audit":{"questions":["Please provide your record of processing activities and explain which entries relate to processing on behalf of customers.","When was this record last updated? How are new projects added?","Which technical and organizational measures apply to this processing activity? How do you show that they are implemented?","Who is responsible for this processing workflow? How is the data finally deleted?","Does the processor record include sub-processors and third-country transfers?","Does the Art. 30(5) GDPR exemption apply to your company? What is the basis for that decision?"],"evidence":["Processor record of processing activities (including version history)","Data flow diagrams or work procedure descriptions","Mapping between the RoPA, the information asset inventory and the protection needs classification","List of TOMs or description of the technical and organizational measures","Records of regular RoPA reviews"],"findings":["Only internal processing such as HR is recorded; there is no processor record at all for processing on behalf of customers.","The RoPA is still the version from two years ago; recently signed OEM projects are missing.","The TOMs in the RoPA only say “according to the company's security policy” and cannot be traced to specific measures or evidence of implementation.","The processor record contains many details of internal processing, or customer data and internal data are mixed in the same row, so responsibilities are unclear."]},"tip":"I often ask customers to do a small test: pick any OEM project and ask the project manager what personal data it involves, where it is stored, who can see it and when it is deleted. If they can answer, the RoPA is alive."},{"id":"9.4.1","title":"Data protection impact assessment (DPIA)","q_en":"To what extent is adequate handling of high-risk processing activities ensured (data protection impact assessment)?","q_zh":"To what extent is adequate handling of high-risk processing activities ensured (data protection impact assessment)?","objective":"The handling of high-risk processing is secured in cooperation with the service provider with appropriate measures to identify and, if necessary, reduce risks to the rights and freedoms of the data subjects.","req":{"must":["Processing activities that require a data protection impact assessment are known.","Data protection impact assessments are carried out. – Responsibilities, tasks and support possibilities in the context of data protection impact assessments are defined and known."],"should":[],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"No reference standards listed in the original | Supplementary mapping: GDPR Art. 35, 36, Art. 28(3)(f) | ISO/IEC 27701 7.2.5, ISO/IEC 29134","change2027":"","explain":["Under Art. 35 GDPR, when processing is likely to result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons, the **controller** must carry out a data protection impact assessment (DPIA) in advance. Typical cases include systematic and extensive evaluation or profiling, large-scale processing of special categories of data, and large-scale systematic monitoring of publicly accessible areas. In the automotive industry, driver-fatigue monitoring with in-cabin cameras, measurement of test drivers' physiological signals, and large-scale analysis of connected-vehicle location data may all be high-risk.","If the DPIA is the controller's duty, why is the processor also asked? Because Art. 28(3)(f) requires the processor to assist the controller in meeting its obligations under Art. 32 to 36, which include the DPIA. The objective also says clearly that high-risk processing is secured “in cooperation with the service provider”. So, as a processor, you must know which of the processing activities your customer has given you require a DPIA, and you must be able to provide information and descriptions of security measures for your part of the work to help the customer complete the assessment.","If you are a controller yourself (for example, if you use biometric access control for employees), you must carry out the DPIA yourself. The sub-point stresses that who is responsible for what in a DPIA, and what support can be given, must be defined in advance and known to the people involved. Do not wait until the customer e-mails you asking for DPIA information before you start looking for the right person.","Supplement: the supervisory authorities of the EU member states publish lists of processing operations that require a DPIA, and the EDPB has published criteria. In practice, “meets two or more high-risk criteria” is often used as the first screening rule. For the formal decision, follow the views of the customer and its supervisory authority."],"practice":["Create a “DPIA Screening Form” to be completed whenever a new project or processing activity starts. Check items include: special categories of data, large-scale processing, systematic monitoring, use of new technologies, matching or combining data sets, vulnerable data subjects and automated decision-making.","Add columns to the RoPA: “DPIA required?”, “carried out by” and “DPIA document number”, so that processing activities that require a DPIA are easy to see.","Write a “Work Instruction for Supporting Customer DPIAs”: named contact (the data protection lead), the document package you can provide (data flow diagrams, description of TOMs, sub-processor list, security certifications) and the response time.","For high-risk processing where your company is the controller, carry out a DPIA using ISO/IEC 29134 or a supervisory authority template. Record the risks, measures, residual risks and management approval.","Link DPIA results to risk management (ISA 1.4.1) and add risk-reduction measures to the improvement tracking list."],"audit":{"questions":["Which processing activities require a DPIA? How do you decide?","Please provide DPIA screening records or completed DPIAs.","When a customer asks for help with a DPIA, who is responsible? What information can you provide?","Where are the records of your most recent support for a customer DPIA?","Are the risk measures identified in the DPIA tracked until they are implemented?"],"evidence":["DPIA screening form or decision list","Completed DPIA reports or DPIA support documents provided to customers","Work instruction and division of responsibilities for supporting customer DPIAs","E-mails or meeting minutes with customers about DPIAs","Risk treatment tracking records"],"findings":["The company processes high-risk data such as in-cabin camera images or biometric data but has never assessed whether a DPIA is needed.","The company believes the DPIA is “the customer's business”, and nobody in the company knows how to support it.","A DPIA document exists, but many template fields are empty and the risk measures are not tracked."]},"tip":"The processor is not the main author of the DPIA. But if you can deliver the data flow diagram and the description of security measures within one week when the customer asks, the OEM's trust in you will grow a lot."},{"id":"9.5.1","title":"Management of data transfers","q_en":"To what extent is the transfer of data managed?","q_zh":"To what extent is the transfer of data managed?","objective":"The company knows and secures data transmissions.","req":{"must":["Appropriate processes and workflows for the transmission of data are implemented (e.g. valid contracts within the meaning of Art. 28 GDPR, suitable transfer instruments like standard contractual clauses, transfer impact assessments, adequacy decisions) – Ensuring the consent or the right of objection of the person responsible for subcontracting"],"should":[],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"No reference standards listed in the original | Supplementary mapping: GDPR Art. 28(2), 28(3), 28(4), Art. 44–49 | ISO/IEC 27701 8.5, ISO 27001:2022 A.5.14, A.5.19, A.5.20","change2027":"","explain":["The core of this control: when personal data moves between organizations, is there a process that stands up legally? The original text lists examples of instruments: a valid Art. 28 processing contract (DPA), transfer instruments such as standard contractual clauses (SCC), transfer impact assessments (TIA) and adequacy decisions. You do not need to invent your own instruments, but for every data transfer you must be able to say which contract or mechanism it is based on.","The sub-point stresses that you must ensure the controller's right to consent to or object to “subcontracting”. Art. 28(2) GDPR says that the processor must not engage another processor without prior specific or general written authorization of the controller. In the case of general authorization, the processor must inform the controller before adding or replacing a sub-processor, so that the controller has the opportunity to object. A common situation at suppliers: test data is uploaded to a new cloud analytics platform, or an external engineer is asked to help with debugging, without first informing the OEM. This breaks this Must.","In practice, a “transfer” is not only sending files by e-mail. It also includes API connections, cloud sharing, handing data to sub-processors and transfers within a corporate group. This control is closely related to ISA 5.1.1 (information transfer) and the 6.1 supplier management controls, but 9.5.1 looks at the legal basis and the controller's consent."],"practice":["Set up a “DPA Register”: customer, contract number, effective date, type of sub-processor authorization (specific or general), notification period, objection period, third-country transfer clauses.","Define a “Sub-processor Onboarding and Change Procedure”: the business unit submits a request, the data protection lead reviews it, the customer is informed according to the DPA, and the sub-processor may only be used after the objection period has expired or written consent has been received.","Maintain a “Sub-processor List” that can be given to customers, including name, location, processing performed and applicable safeguards.","Check before each data transfer: is the recipient on the authorized list, is the channel encrypted (SFTP, TLS, encrypted archives), and does the transfer follow the data minimization principle?","Add a checkbox to the purchasing process (e.g. the ERP purchase requisition): “Does this purchase involve processing customer personal data?” Ticking it triggers a data protection review."],"audit":{"questions":["Where are the DPAs with each customer? Please provide the register.","When you add a sub-processor, how do you obtain the customer's consent or give the customer the opportunity to object?","Where is the record of the last time you informed a customer about a change of sub-processor?","Which sub-processors do you currently use? Are your customers aware of them?","Which channels and protection measures do you use when you transfer data to sub-processors?"],"evidence":["DPA register and copies of contracts","Sub-processor list","E-mails informing customers about new or changed sub-processors, or written consent","Sub-processor Onboarding and Change Procedure","Configuration of data transfer channels or proof of encryption"],"findings":["A new cloud service is used to process customer personal data without the customer's consent.","No DPA has been signed with some customers, or the DPA has expired and was not renewed.","The sub-processor list does not match the services actually used; for example, an overseas IT operations contractor is missing."]},"tip":"The classic finding I have seen: to meet a deadline, an engineer uploaded customer data to a free online file-conversion tool. That tool is also a sub-processor, and the customer knew nothing about it."},{"id":"9.5.2","title":"Passing contractual obligations to sub-processors","q_en":"To what extent are contractual obligations passed through to and enforced at subcontractors and cooperation partners?","q_zh":"To what extent are contractual obligations passed through to and enforced at subcontractors and cooperation partners?","objective":"The company is aware of and secures data transfers to subcontractors.","req":{"must":["Applicable contractual obligations to clients are passed on to subcontractors and cooperation partners (sub-processors).","Compliance with contractual agreements is reviewed. – Contact details of the contact persons of the subcontractor are available and up to date."],"should":[],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"No reference standards listed in the original | Supplementary mapping: GDPR Art. 28(4), 28(3)(h) | ISO/IEC 27701 8.5.7, 8.5.8, ISO 27001:2022 A.5.19, A.5.20, A.5.22","change2027":"","explain":["Art. 28(4) GDPR says that when a processor engages another processor, it must impose **the same data protection obligations** as in the original contract on the sub-processor by contract. If the sub-processor fails to meet them, the original processor remains fully liable to the controller. This is the first Must in 9.5.2: the obligations the OEM gives you must be “passed down”.","Typical obligations to pass down: processing only on instructions, confidentiality, security measures, restrictions on further subcontracting, support with data subject requests, incident notification deadlines, audit rights, and deletion or return of data at the end of the service. If the OEM requires you to report incidents within 24 hours, but your contract with the sub-processor says 7 days, you cannot possibly keep your promise to the OEM.","The second Must is “review compliance”: you cannot sign the contract and then forget it. You can check through questionnaires, by requesting ISO 27001 certificates or TISAX results, or through on-site or remote audits. The sub-point requires that the subcontractor's contact details are available and up to date, so that you can reach someone when an incident happens.","This control is related to ISA 6.1.1 and 6.1.2 (supplier management). The difference is that 9.5.2 focuses on whether data protection obligations are “passed on equally” and “reviewed in practice”."],"practice":["Create a “Customer DPA Obligation Matrix” that lists the key obligations of each customer DPA and checks whether each sub-processor contract contains the same or stricter clauses.","Create a sub-processor DPA template based on the strictest customer requirements, for example incident notification deadlines, data location, deletion periods and audit rights.","Evaluate sub-processors every year: send a data protection questionnaire, collect ISO 27001, ISO 27701 or TISAX results, and audit high-risk sub-processors. Record the results in the supplier evaluation form.","Maintain a sub-processor contact list with: company, business contact, security or data protection contact, 24-hour emergency contact, date of last confirmation. Confirm it every six months.","Open an improvement ticket for each non-conformity found and track it until it is closed."],"audit":{"questions":["Does the sub-processor contract contain the same obligations as the customer DPA? Please explain using one sub-processor as an example.","How do you check that sub-processors actually comply with the contract? What was the result of the last review?","Who are the sub-processor's contact persons? How often is this information updated?","If a sub-processor has a personal data breach, how quickly must it notify you?","How are issues found during reviews tracked and corrected?"],"evidence":["Sub-processor DPAs or contract clauses","Customer DPA obligation matrix","Sub-processor questionnaires, audit reports or certificates","Sub-processor contact list and update records","Improvement tracking records"],"findings":["There is only a general purchasing contract with the sub-processor, with no data protection clauses at all.","The incident notification deadline in the sub-processor contract is looser than the customer's requirement, so the commitment to the customer cannot be met.","The sub-processor has never been reviewed since the contract was signed, and the contact person left long ago."]},"tip":"Use the customer DPA as a checklist and go down it line by line: whatever the customer requires from you, you require from your sub-processor. You may be stricter, but never looser."},{"id":"9.5.3","title":"Transfers to third countries","q_en":"To what extent are data transfers to third countries managed?","q_zh":"To what extent are data transfers to third countries managed?","objective":"The company is aware of and secures data transfers to third countries.","req":{"must":["Transfers to third countries are known and systematically recorded. – e.g. through corresponding documentation in the processing directory","Sufficient guarantees (Chapter V GDPR, consideration of decisions of the ECJ on international data transfer, Transfer Impact Assessment in case of relevance, especially in the role of data exporter) are available for data transfers.","In the case of data transfers to third countries, it is determined whether the consent of the person responsible is to be obtained for each transfer to third countries."],"should":[],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"No reference standards listed in the original | Supplementary mapping: GDPR Chapter V Art. 44–49, Art. 28(3)(a) | ECJ C-311/18 (Schrems II) | ISO/IEC 27701 8.5.1–8.5.3","change2027":"","explain":["A “third country” is any country outside the EU and the European Economic Area (EEA). Check the European Commission's current list of adequacy decisions: some countries have one (for example Japan, South Korea, the United Kingdom and Switzerland), while many supplier locations do not (for example China, India, Mexico or Taiwan, at the time of writing). If your country has no adequacy decision, a transfer of personal data from a German OEM to you is already a third-country transfer. It is usually covered by standard contractual clauses (SCC) between the OEM (data exporter) and you (data importer). If you then pass the data on to a subsidiary, a cloud region or a plant in another country, this is an “onward transfer”: you become the data exporter, and your responsibility grows. Suppliers located inside the EU become data exporters as soon as they pass data to a sub-processor or support team outside the EU and EEA.","First Must: third-country transfers must be “known and systematically recorded”. The most direct way is to include them in the record of processing activities (9.3.1) and to note for each processing activity the countries where the data is stored and accessed. Do not forget remote access: for example, an overseas IT contractor who logs in to a server remotely to look at data.","Second Must: sufficient guarantees. The instruments in Chapter V GDPR include adequacy decisions (Art. 45), appropriate safeguards such as SCC or binding corporate rules (Art. 46 and 47), and derogations for specific situations (Art. 49). The requirement asks you to take the decisions of the European Court of Justice into account. The best known is the 2020 Schrems II judgment: the court said that even when SCC are used, you must assess whether the law of the destination country provides essentially equivalent protection. This is where the transfer impact assessment (TIA) comes from. The original text points out that a TIA is especially relevant when you are the **data exporter**.","Third Must: for each third-country transfer, determine whether the controller's consent is needed. Under Art. 28(3)(a), the processor may only process personal data on the controller's documented instructions, and this includes transfers to third countries. Many OEM DPAs state clearly that data may only be stored in certain regions, and adding any new country requires prior written consent."],"practice":["Add columns to the RoPA: country of storage, countries of access (including remote operations and technical support), recipient, transfer instrument (adequacy decision, SCC module, BCR), TIA document number, and whether customer consent has been obtained.","Check the data center regions of your cloud services, for example the tenant region settings of M365, AWS or Azure and the locations from which support staff can access data. Keep screenshots or copies of the terms of service.","When you are the data exporter, sign the European Commission's SCC with your sub-processors and carry out a TIA that assesses the risk of government access in the destination country and the supplementary measures (e.g. encryption, pseudonymization, keys managed by yourself).","Define a “Third-Country Transfer Approval Procedure”: before any transfer to a new country or location, the data protection lead confirms whether the DPA allows it and whether customer consent is needed. The transfer may only start after consent has been obtained.","Provide customers with a data location statement and inform them proactively when it changes."],"audit":{"questions":["In which countries is customer personal data stored or accessed?","Which transfer instrument do you use with the customer, for example which SCC module?","If data is transferred onward to other countries, have you carried out a TIA? Please provide it.","How do you decide whether a third-country transfer requires customer consent? Where is the record of the most recent consent?","Is remote access by overseas IT contractors included in your third-country transfer analysis?"],"evidence":["Record of processing activities with third-country columns","Contracts for SCC or other transfer instruments","Transfer impact assessment (TIA) reports","Written records of customer consent to third-country transfers","Screenshots of cloud service data center region settings"],"findings":["The company does not know where its cloud services store data, and the RoPA has no third-country columns.","Customer personal data is transferred onward to an overseas subsidiary without SCC or a TIA.","The DPA limits data storage to the EU, but a copy is kept on a local server in the supplier's home country outside the EU without customer consent."]},"tip":"Many suppliers think there is no cross-border issue because “the data stays in our country”. But from the EU's point of view, your country may already be a third country. If you pass the data on again, it becomes a third country of a third country."}],"summary":["Chapter 9 only applies to processing on behalf of a customer within the meaning of Art. 28 GDPR. The controls only have Must requirements and are answered yes or no; an isolated assessment of the data protection module cannot lead to a TISAX label.","The controller determines the purposes and means of processing; the processor processes on its behalf. Suppliers that process personal data for EU OEMs are usually processors.","Common labels: Data (ordinary personal data) and Special data (special categories under Art. 9 GDPR); both must be assessed together with the information security controls.","9.1.1: The data protection policy must be created, regularly updated and approved by management, and must cover processing on behalf of customers.","9.2.1: Determine whether a DPO is mandatory, otherwise assign a data protection function; publish the contact details, integrate the role into the organization, perform monitoring, report to top management, and provide enough resources and training.","9.3.1: Create and maintain a record of processing activities under Art. 30 GDPR; the processor record contains only information relating to the order; TOMs must be implemented and the process must define responsibilities.","9.4.1: Know which processing activities need a DPIA, carry out DPIAs, and define responsibilities and support for assisting the controller.","9.5.1–9.5.3: Data transfers need a basis such as a DPA, SCC or a TIA; the controller must be able to consent to or object to subcontracting; obligations must be passed down and reviewed; third-country transfers must be recorded and safeguarded, and you must determine whether customer consent is needed.","Supplement: many national data-protection laws have similar concepts. For example, Taiwan's PDPA distinguishes the commissioning agency from the commissioned party, and Art. 8 of its Enforcement Rules requires supervision of the commissioned party, which is close to Art. 28 GDPR. Check how your own local law handles this."]},{"id":"dp-2","title":"Chapter 9 (2) — Data Protection: Requests, Incidents, Human Resources and Instructions","title_en":"Data Protection (2): Requests, Incidents, Human Resources and Instructions","intro":["The second part of Chapter 9 focuses on the four areas where processors most often “get into trouble” in daily operations: **data subject requests**, **personal data breaches**, **people** and **the controller's instructions**. These are all obligations the processor owes the controller under Art. 28(3) GDPR: assisting with data subject requests (point e), assisting the controller with security and breach notification obligations (point f), ensuring that the persons who process the data are bound to confidentiality (point b), and processing only on documented instructions (point a).","A reminder on roles: **replying to data subject requests** (Art. 12 GDPR generally requires a reply within one month) and **notifying the supervisory authority within 72 hours** (Art. 33(1)) are the controller's responsibilities. The processor's responsibility is to make this possible for the controller: forward requests to the controller immediately, notify the controller **without undue delay** after a personal data breach (Art. 33(2)), and provide the information and help needed to handle the incident. In practice, OEM DPAs often agree on shorter notification deadlines; always follow the contract.","Supplement — your local law: most national data-protection laws contain similar ideas, so compare the GDPR with your own law. Taiwan's PDPA is one example: Art. 3 gives data subjects the rights to inquire, review, obtain copies, supplement or correct, stop collection, processing or use, and request deletion; Art. 12 requires the data subjects to be notified in an appropriate way after the facts are clarified when data is stolen, leaked, altered or otherwise infringed; and Art. 8 of the Enforcement Rules requires the commissioning party to agree with the commissioned party on notification and remedial measures in case of violations, and allows it to reserve the right to give instructions. These concepts are very similar to the GDPR processor obligations, so one set of processes can usually meet both.","The controls in this chapter also only have Must requirements, and the result is yes or no."],"blocks":[{"type":"h3","text":"The processor's role in requests and incidents"},{"type":"table","head":["Situation","Controller (OEM)","Processor (you)"],"rows":[["Data subject exercises rights","Decides on and replies to the data subject","Forwards immediately, does not reply on its own, helps to search, correct or delete as instructed"],["Personal data breach","Assesses it and notifies the supervisory authority within 72 hours; notifies data subjects if needed","Notifies the controller without undue delay, provides incident information, records the handling, supports the investigation"],["Processing instructions","Gives and records instructions","Records instructions, is able to carry them out, separates data by customer and project"]]},{"type":"olist","items":["Detect: an employee, the SOC or an EDR alert detects a suspected personal data breach.","Report: report to the security contact and the data protection lead according to the ISA 1.6 incident procedure.","Assess: confirm whether personal data is involved and which customers' data is affected.","Notify the controller: notify the customer within the DPA deadline, giving the known facts and a contact person; if information is incomplete, add it in stages.","Handle and support: contain the incident, preserve evidence, answer the customer's questions and support the investigation.","Record and improve: complete the incident record, root-cause analysis and corrective actions, and add the case to the training material."]},{"type":"callout","style":"warn","title":"Do not reply to data subjects on the customer's behalf","text":"As a processor, when a data subject writes to you asking for their data to be deleted or disclosed, **do not reply yourself or delete the data directly**. Inform the responsible controller immediately and agree on the next steps. This is exactly what 9.6.1 requires employees to be trained on."},{"type":"tip","who":"arale","text":"Note it down! The processor's three don'ts: don't reply to data subjects yourself, don't process beyond the instructions, and don't delay notifying the controller."}],"controls":[{"id":"9.6.1","title":"Handling data subject requests","q_en":"To what extent are data subject requests processed?","q_zh":"To what extent are data subject requests processed?","objective":"The objective is to ensure the timely processing and fulfilment of data subject requests in order to secure the rights of data subjects guaranteed by law.","req":{"must":["Requests from data subjects are processed in a timely manner. – Procedures are in place to assist the controller in responding to data subject requests. – Employees are trained to the effect that they must immediately contact the respective person responsible in the event of an incoming request from a data subject and coordinate the further procedure with this person."],"should":[],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"No reference standards listed in the original | Supplementary mapping: GDPR Art. 12–22, Art. 28(3)(e) | ISO/IEC 27701 7.3, 8.3.1","change2027":"","explain":["Articles 15 to 22 GDPR give data subjects several rights: access, rectification, erasure, restriction of processing, data portability, objection, and the right not to be subject to decisions based solely on automated processing. Under Art. 12, the controller must generally reply within one month. The processor does not send the reply, but Art. 28(3)(e) requires it to assist the controller with appropriate technical and organizational measures in meeting these obligations.","The Must in 9.6.1 has two key points. First, you need procedures to assist the controller. For example, if the customer asks you to find all data about a certain test driver in your systems and export or delete it, you must be able to do this within a reasonable time and provide a record of what you did. This requires knowing where the data is, which means a good record of processing activities (9.3.1).","Second, employees must be trained: when they receive a data subject request, they must contact the responsible controller **immediately** and agree on the next steps. In practice, data subjects may contact you through many channels: the customer service mailbox, a sales contact, or even LinkedIn. If front-line staff do not recognize a legal request and the e-mail sits for two weeks before being forwarded, you have used up half of the controller's one-month deadline.","Also note that the processor should not decide on and reply to a request by itself, because whether the request is granted and whether an exception applies is the controller's decision. If the processor deletes data on its own, it may also break the controller's retention obligations."],"practice":["Write a “Procedure for Assisting with Data Subject Requests”: intake channels, how to recognize a request, the deadline for forwarding it to the controller (for example within one working day), the steps to help search, correct, delete or export data, and how to report and record completion.","Keep a “Data Subject Request Log” with: date received, channel, type of request, customer concerned, time forwarded, customer instruction, completion date and handler.","Prepare technical capabilities for each customer system: search all data by data subject identifier, export it in common formats, delete it and keep a deletion record, and describe how backups are handled.","Put a simple rule next to the customer service mailbox, switchboard and sales contacts: if someone mentions “my data”, “delete” or “what personal data do you have about me”, forward it to the data protection lead.","Train once a year and run exercises with simulated request e-mails to check whether front-line staff forward them within the deadline."],"audit":{"questions":["If a data subject asks you to delete their data, what is your process?","Please provide your data subject request log or handling records.","How do employees know that they must inform the controller immediately? Are there training records?","If a customer asks you to help export all data about a person, how long does it take you?","Do deletion requests also cover backups and data held by sub-processors?"],"evidence":["Procedure for Assisting with Data Subject Requests","Data subject request log","Employee training material and attendance or online completion records","Records of coordination with customers on requests","Description or logs of system search, export and deletion functions"],"findings":["There is no procedure, and employees do not know whom to contact when they receive a data subject request.","The processor replied to the data subject and deleted the data on its own, without instructions from the controller.","The request records do not show when the request was forwarded to the controller, so timely handling cannot be shown."]},"tip":"Front-line staff do not need to know every article of the GDPR. They only need to remember one sentence: if someone asks about their own data, forward it to the data protection contact at once and do not reply yourself."},{"id":"9.6.2","title":"Handling personal data breaches","q_en":"To what extent are data protection incidents processed?","q_zh":"To what extent are data protection incidents processed?","objective":"The objective of processing data protection incidents is to ensure that possible damage to the data subjects is limited and that a recurrence is prevented. In addition, the legally required documentation and, if necessary, timely reporting to the supervisory authority must be ensured.","req":{"must":["Data protection incidents (e.g. unauthorized access to personal data) are processed in a timely manner.","The requirements from 1.6 of the information security questionnaire also take into account data protection incidents or, alternatively, there is an emergency plan for dealing with data protection incidents.","In addition, procedures are established and documented to ensure the following aspects: – Immediate notification to the respective responsible person, as far as his order is affected – Documentation of the incident handling activities – Training of employees on the defined measures and processes – Support of the respective controller in the processing of data protection incidents"],"should":[],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"No reference standards listed in the original | Supplementary mapping: GDPR Art. 33, 34, Art. 28(3)(f) | ISO/IEC 27701 6.13, ISO 27001:2022 A.5.24–A.5.28","change2027":"","explain":["This control links directly to section 1.6 (security incident management) of the information security questionnaire. The Must offers two options: include personal data breaches in the 1.6 incident management process, or create a separate emergency plan for personal data breaches. For most small and medium-sized companies, we recommend the first option: add a “personal data involved?” decision point to your existing incident classification, so that two processes do not conflict.","The third Must lists four aspects that need documented procedures: **immediate notification of the controller**, **documentation of the handling**, **training of employees** and **support of the controller**. The 72-hour notification to the supervisory authority in Art. 33(1) GDPR is the controller's duty; Art. 33(2) requires the processor to notify the controller without undue delay after becoming aware of the breach. The controller's 72 hours usually start when the controller becomes aware, but if you delay your notification, the controller may run out of time. That is why OEM DPAs often agree on 24 or 48 hours. Always follow the contract.","Documentation is also important. Art. 33(5) GDPR requires the controller to document all personal data breaches, including the facts, the effects and the remedial action taken, and the processor's records are a main source for this. Your records should include the time of detection, the time the controller was notified, the categories and number of personal data records affected, the measures taken and the root-cause analysis.","Supplement: national data-protection laws often add their own notification duties, so check your local law. For example, Taiwan's PDPA Art. 12 requires the data subjects to be notified in an appropriate way after the facts are clarified, and if you are also a commissioned party under Taiwanese law, the supervision agreement under Art. 8 of the Enforcement Rules usually requires you to notify the commissioning party of violations. One process can usually cover both the GDPR and your local law."],"practice":["Revise the ISA 1.6 “Security Incident Management Procedure” to add a “personal data breach” category and decision questions: Is personal data involved? Which customer orders are affected? Are special categories of data involved?","Create a “Customer Incident Notification Matrix”: each customer's DPA notification deadline, contact and format (e-mail, customer portal, phone). Keep it where the incident response team can find it immediately.","Prepare a “Personal Data Breach Notification Template”: summary of the incident, time of detection, data subjects and data categories possibly affected, measures already taken, and the contact for follow-up. If information is incomplete, it can be added in stages.","Create a “Personal Data Breach Record” and link it to SIEM, EDR or DLP alert ticket numbers to keep the chain of evidence.","Run at least one tabletop exercise per year, for example: an employee sends an Excel file with test-driver data to the wrong supplier. Practice the whole process from detection to customer notification and measure the time.","Anonymize incident cases and add them to the annual data protection training material."],"audit":{"questions":["Please describe your process when a personal data breach occurs.","How does your incident management procedure cover personal data breaches?","What notification deadlines do your customers require? How do you make sure you meet them?","Please provide the record of your most recent personal data breach or exercise.","Have employees been trained on reporting personal data breaches?","When a customer investigates an incident, what support and information can you provide?"],"evidence":["Incident management procedure that covers personal data breaches, or a personal data breach response plan","Customer incident notification matrix and notification template","Personal data breach records or exercise reports","Incident-related training records","Records of incident notifications to customers"],"findings":["The incident management procedure does not mention personal data at all, and there is no separate personal data breach response plan.","An e-mail containing customer personal data was sent to the wrong recipient; it was handled internally, but the customer was never informed.","The company does not know the notification deadlines agreed in each customer DPA and believes that the processor must also notify the supervisory authority within 72 hours.","There is no record of the incident handling, so the timeline and the actions taken cannot be explained."]},"tip":"The golden rule for personal data breaches: notify the customer first, then investigate in detail. If you notify late, what the customer cares about most is not that you were hacked, but that you kept it hidden."},{"id":"9.7.1","title":"Employee confidentiality obligations","q_en":"To what extent are employees obliged to maintain confidentiality?","q_zh":"To what extent are employees obliged to maintain confidentiality?","objective":"Organizations are subject to laws, regulations, and internal policies. For employment (hiring, implementation, termination), it is important that employees commit to compliance with the guidelines.","req":{"must":["Employees whose tasks include the processing of personal data are obliged to maintain confidentiality (even beyond the duration of the employment relationship) and to comply with applicable data protection laws. – The obligation is documented."],"should":[],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"No reference standards listed in the original | Supplementary mapping: GDPR Art. 28(3)(b), Art. 29, Art. 32(4) | ISO 27001:2022 A.6.1, A.6.2, A.6.6","change2027":"","explain":["Art. 28(3)(b) GDPR requires the processor to ensure that persons authorized to process personal data have committed themselves to confidentiality or are under a statutory obligation of confidentiality. 9.7.1 makes this concrete in three points: first, employees who process personal data must sign a confidentiality commitment; second, it remains valid **after they leave**; third, besides confidentiality, they must also commit to complying with applicable data protection laws. All of this must be **documented**.","Many companies already have a confidentiality clause in their employment contracts. The common problems are that the clause only covers “trade secrets” and does not mention personal data or data protection laws, or that it does not state that it remains valid after employment ends. We recommend a separate “Personal Data Confidentiality and Compliance Declaration”, or adding personal data clauses to the existing NDA.","Also pay attention to who is covered. The requirement refers to “employees whose tasks include the processing of personal data”. In practice, we recommend including temporary agency workers, interns and on-site contractor engineers, because they also handle customer data. For external staff, this can be done through ISA 2.1.2 or supplier contracts. This control is related to the ISA 2.1 controls (personnel security), and you can use the same onboarding and offboarding process."],"practice":["Create a “Personal Data Confidentiality and Compliance Declaration” covering: the scope of confidentiality (including customer personal data processed on the customer's behalf), compliance with the GDPR, your national data-protection law and company policies, continued validity after employment ends, and consequences of violations.","Add the signing of the declaration to the HR onboarding checklist. IT does not grant access to accounts with customer data until it is signed.","Review existing employees, have those who process personal data sign the declaration, and keep a signature register (name, department, date signed, version).","During offboarding, remind the employee that the confidentiality obligation continues, and have them sign a leaving statement confirming that data has been returned and deleted.","Apply the same rules to temporary agency workers, interns and on-site contractors, or require in the outsourcing contract that their staff sign an equivalent confidentiality commitment."],"audit":{"questions":["Do employees who process personal data sign a confidentiality commitment? Please provide the template and the signature register.","Does the confidentiality obligation remain valid after employment ends? Where is this stated?","Do employees also commit to complying with data protection laws?","When do new employees sign? Can they access customer data before they sign?","How do you handle temporary agency workers or on-site contractors?"],"evidence":["Confidentiality declaration or personal data clause in the employment contract","Employee signature register and sampled signed originals","Onboarding and offboarding checklists","Confidentiality commitments or contract clauses for external staff"],"findings":["The confidentiality clause only covers trade secrets and does not mention personal data or data protection laws.","A sample check shows that a newly hired engineer has had access to customer test data for several months without signing a confidentiality commitment.","The clause does not state that it remains valid after employment ends."]},"tip":"Auditors love sampling: they take three people from the list of accounts with access to customer data and ask HR for their signed declarations. So your register must match the account list."},{"id":"9.7.2","title":"Data protection training for employees","q_en":"To what extent are employees trained in data protection?","q_zh":"To what extent are employees trained in data protection?","objective":"If the requirements and risks of data protection are not known to employees, there is a risk that employees will behave incorrectly and thus damage the organization. It is therefore important that data protection is internalized and lived as a natural part of their work.","req":{"must":["Employees are trained and sensitized. – Scope, frequency, and content of the training is determined according to the protection needs of the data – Employees in critical areas (e.g. IT administrators) are instructed and trained specifically for their work (e.g. specific training courses or instructions, short videos, etc.)."],"should":[],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"No reference standards listed in the original | Supplementary mapping: GDPR Art. 39(1)(b), Art. 32(4) | ISO/IEC 27701 6.4.2.2, ISO 27001:2022 A.6.3","change2027":"","explain":["9.7.2 has two levels. First, all relevant employees must be trained and made aware, and the scope, frequency and content of the training must depend on the **protection needs of the data**. A project team that processes special categories of data (such as health or biometric data) needs deeper and more frequent training than general administrative staff.","Second, employees in **critical areas** need training specific to their work. The original text gives IT administrators as an example. IT administrators have high privileges and can see databases, backups and logs; one wrong command or a little curiosity can cause a large-scale breach. Other critical roles may include data analysts, customer service staff, HR, and data collection staff at test sites. The format is flexible: dedicated courses, work instructions and short videos are all acceptable.","This control is related to ISA 2.1.3 (security awareness and training) and can be combined with the annual security training. But you must be able to show that the training is designed “according to protection needs” and “for critical roles”, not just one video that the whole company watches."],"practice":["Create a “Data Protection Training Plan” as a matrix: target group (all staff, project members who process customer personal data, IT administrators, customer service, HR), course content, frequency, format and completion criteria.","Course for all staff: basic data protection concepts, the processor's three don'ts, and reporting channels for incidents and data subject requests. Once a year, as an online course with a quiz.","Members of projects with high protection needs: key points of the project DPA, data minimization and de-identification, data separation and deletion. At project start and once a year.","IT administrators: rules for privileged accounts, approval and logging of access to customer data, backup and deletion tasks, and preservation of incident evidence. Provide work instructions and short videos.","Track completion rates and quiz results in an LMS or with M365 Forms. Remind the manager of anyone who has not completed the training and restrict their access.","Strengthen awareness with social engineering exercises, tabletop exercises and case sharing."],"audit":{"questions":["How do you decide the target groups, content and frequency of data protection training?","Do teams that process special categories of data receive additional training?","What specific training or work instructions do IT administrators have?","Please provide training completion rates and quiz records.","How do you handle employees who have not completed the training?"],"evidence":["Data protection training plan or training matrix","Course material, work instructions and short videos","LMS completion records and quiz results","Specific training records for IT administrators","Follow-up records for employees who have not completed the training"],"findings":["The whole company has only one general security video, with no differences based on data protection needs.","IT administrators have no specific data protection training or instructions.","Training completion rates are low with no follow-up, and new employees are not trained when they join."]},"tip":"Training is not finished when the video ends. I suggest that auditors pick any IT colleague and ask: “If the customer asks you to delete a person's data, what do you do?” If they can explain the process, the training works."},{"id":"9.8.1","title":"Handling processing instructions","q_en":"To what extent are instructions of processing relationships handled?","q_zh":"To what extent are instructions of processing relationships handled?","objective":"The orderly and defined handling of instructions with regard to the processing of the controller is intended to ensure that the tasks of the processor are fulfilled, and that the processor fulfils the planned and contractually agreed obligations (in particular also to determine a possible exceeding of the contractually agreed framework).","req":{"must":["The instructions by the controller regarding the processing of personal data are handled.","Procedures and measures are in place to ensure that: – Received instructions are documented – Instructions can be implemented (e.g. procedures for correcting, deleting, ...) – Data is separated by client and specific order or project"],"should":[],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"No reference standards listed in the original | Supplementary mapping: GDPR Art. 28(3)(a), Art. 28(10), Art. 29 | ISO/IEC 27701 8.2.2, ISO 27001:2022 A.5.12, A.8.3","change2027":"","explain":["Art. 28(3)(a) and Art. 29 GDPR state that the processor may only process personal data on the controller's **documented instructions**. If the processor goes beyond the instructions and decides the purposes and means of processing itself, Art. 28(10) says it is considered the controller for that processing and bears all of the controller's responsibilities. The goal of 9.8.1 is to help you notice when you may be “exceeding the contractually agreed framework”.","The second Must lists three measures. **Document the instructions**: the DPA itself is the main instruction, but later e-mails, meeting minutes and changes in tickets are also instructions; keep them in one place and make them traceable. **Instructions can be implemented**: if the customer asks you to correct, delete, export or stop processing, you must have the technology and procedures to do it. **Separate data by customer and project**: this is what auditors most often check on site, for example whether folders of different OEMs on a shared file server have separate permissions, whether databases are separated by schema or tenant, and whether test environments mix real data from several customers.","Supplement: national laws often contain the same principle. For example, Art. 7 of the Enforcement Rules of Taiwan's PDPA says the commissioned party may only process personal data within the scope of the commissioning party's instructions and must notify it immediately if it believes an instruction violates the law. Art. 28(3) GDPR also requires the processor to inform the controller immediately if it believes an instruction infringes the GDPR. Whatever your local law is, this idea is worth including in your procedure.","This control is related to data separation in ISA 5.2.x, access control (4.x) and asset management (1.3). Good data separation also makes the searches and deletions in 9.6.1 easier."],"practice":["Keep a “Customer Processing Instruction Log”: customer, project, source of the instruction (DPA, e-mail, ticket), content, date received, handler, status, completion date and customer confirmation.","Accept instructions only through official channels (DPA, designated e-mail address, customer portal). Confirm verbal instructions by e-mail after the meeting.","Define what to do if an instruction is unlawful or outside the contract: stop carrying it out and inform the customer and the data protection lead immediately.","Data separation: create separate spaces and AD groups per customer and project on file servers or SharePoint; separate databases by schema or tenant per customer; label backups by customer and project; do not use real personal data in test environments unless the customer has agreed.","Define a deletion and return procedure: at project end, delete or return the data according to the DPA, including backups and data held by sub-processors, and provide a certificate of deletion.","Review folder and group permissions once a year to confirm that there is no access across customers."],"audit":{"questions":["How do you receive and record customer processing instructions? Please provide the log.","If a customer instructs you to delete the data of a project, how do you do it and how do you prove it?","How is the data of different customers and projects separated? Please show us on site.","What do you do if you believe a customer instruction is unlawful or outside the contract?","How is data returned or deleted at the end of a project?"],"evidence":["Customer processing instruction log with related e-mails and tickets","Data separation design, screenshots of folder permissions or AD groups","Deletion or return records and certificates of deletion","Procedure for handling processing instructions","Permission review records"],"findings":["Customer instructions are scattered across the project managers' mailboxes, with no central record, so they cannot be traced.","Data of different OEMs is stored at the same level of a shared folder, and all engineers can access it.","Projects were closed years ago, but customer personal data is still on servers and in backups and was not deleted as instructed.","To make development easier, an engineer used real data from customer A to test customer B's system."]},"tip":"The biggest risk for a processor is doing extra out of good will. If the customer did not ask for analysis and you analyze the data anyway, you turn from processor into controller, and all the responsibility lands on you at once."}],"summary":["9.6.1: Data subject requests must be processed in a timely manner; the processor needs procedures to assist the controller, and employees who receive a request must contact the controller immediately and not reply themselves.","9.6.2: Personal data breaches must be handled in a timely manner, either within ISA 1.6 incident management or through a separate emergency plan; notify the controller immediately, record the handling, train employees and support the controller.","Notifying the supervisory authority within 72 hours is the controller's duty; under Art. 33(2) GDPR the processor must notify the controller without undue delay, following the deadline agreed in the DPA.","9.7.1: Employees who process personal data must sign a documented commitment to confidentiality and to compliance with the law, which remains valid after they leave.","9.7.2: The scope, frequency and content of training depend on the protection needs of the data; key roles such as IT administrators need specific training or instructions.","9.8.1: Controller instructions must be recorded and must be possible to carry out, and data must be separated by customer and project; processing beyond the instructions can turn the processor into a controller."]}]});
